\title[The quadratic-residue dial of $x^2-N$]{The Quadratic-Residue Dial of $x^2-N$:\\
Exact Local Statistics, an Ensemble Null,\\ and a Uniform Dispersion Ceiling}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{29 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
The quadratic sieve draws its relations from values of $f(x)=x^{2}-N$, and
folklore holds that this polynomial is \emph{better than random} for smoothness,
because it is divisible by a prime $p$ on two residue classes whenever $N$ is a
quadratic residue mod $p$, versus one class for a generic integer. We show that
this folklore is exactly false at the level of first moments, exactly true at the
level of second moments, and that the second-moment effect is bounded by an
absolute constant independent of the smoothness bound.

Define the \emph{dial} $D_p(N)=\#\{x\in\ZZ/p: x^{2}=N\}$ and the induced
\emph{local factor} $L_p(N)=(p-D_p(N))/(p-1)$, the ratio of the local
non-divisibility density of $f$ to that of a random integer. We prove: the dial
dichotomy $D_p\in\{0,1,2\}$ with the exact character identity $D_p=1+\chi_p$; the
exact moments $\sum_N D_p(N)=p$ and $\sum_N D_p(N)^{2}=2p-1$, whence
$\EE[L_p]=1$ exactly and $\Var(L_p)=1/(p(p-1))$ exactly; that for the
\emph{structure correction} $C(\Nbf)=\prod_{p\le B}L_p(N_p)$ the ensemble mean is
exactly $1$ for every finite family of odd primes and the second moment is
exactly $\Delta(a)=\prod_p\bigl(1+\tfrac1{p(p-1)}\bigr)$; the \emph{uniform
ceiling} $1<\Delta(a)\le 2$ valid for every finite family of distinct odd primes
and hence for every smoothness bound; \emph{exact joint uniformity} of the dial
vector on $\{0,2\}^{k}$; and a \emph{dispersion decay theorem}: in any mixed
count model with conditional mean $\lambda C$ and conditional variance
$\lambda C(1-qC)$, $|\Var-\mathrm{Mean}|\le \mathrm{Mean}\cdot(\lambda+2q)$.

These results explain and quantitatively bound a large computational experiment
at $B=1000$ and $u=\log v/\log B\in\{5,6,7,8\}$ comparing the $B$-smoothness of
$x^{2}-N$ against histogram-matched random controls: the measured ratios
$r(u)=1.011,\,0.949,\,0.900,\,1.200$ at $u\approx 5.96,\,6.95,\,7.93,\,8.26$ all
have confidence intervals covering $1$, with tightest bound $|r-1|\le0.217$, and
the observed per-$N$ overdispersion $1.61\,[1.50,1.73]$ at $u\approx6$ together
with its disappearance above $u\approx7$ are consequences of the ceiling and the
decay theorem respectively.
\end{abstract}

