\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The relation-collection bottleneck}
Every subexponential integer factoring algorithm in the index-calculus family
spends the bulk of its time finding many integers in a prescribed algebraic
family that are $B$-smooth, i.e.\ have no prime factor exceeding $B$. In the
quadratic sieve the family is the set of values
\[
f(x)=x^{2}-N,\qquad x=\lceil\sqrt N\rceil,\ \lceil\sqrt N\rceil+1,\dots
\]
Since $f(x)\equiv x^{2}\pmod N$, a multiplicative combination of smooth values
whose prime exponents are all even yields a congruence of squares
$X^{2}\equiv Y^{2}\pmod N$, and $\gcd(X-Y,N)$ is a nontrivial factor with
probability at least $1/2$.

Complexity analysis rests on a heuristic: that the smoothness probability of
$f(x)$ is that of a random integer of the same size. The Dickman--de Bruijn
function $\rho$ gives the density of $B$-smooth integers up to $v$ as
$\rho(u)\approx u^{-u}$ with $u=\log v/\log B$, and the heuristic asserts this
applies to the polynomial values.

\subsection{The folklore edge}
The heuristic looks pessimistic. Reduce $f$ modulo a prime $p$: a random integer
is divisible by $p$ on a $1/p$ fraction of arguments, but $x^{2}-N\equiv0\pmod p$
has two solutions $x\equiv\pm r$ whenever $N\equiv r^{2}$ is a nonzero quadratic
residue. Sieve implementations exploit this directly, restricting the factor base
to primes with $\left(\frac Np\right)=1$ because the others contribute nothing.

The question answered here is whether that doubling amounts to a genuine
smoothness advantage --- an $O(1)$ multiplicative gain over random integers of the
same size --- and if not, what the observable arithmetic signature of the
doubling actually is.

\subsection{The experimental frontier}
A large computational experiment tested this at scale. With $B=1000$ fixed,
candidates $v=|x^{2}-N|$ with $N\le 2^{80}$ and $x\le4\sqrt N$ were binned by
$u=\log v/\log B$ around $u=5,6,7,8$ and compared against controls
histogram-matched on bit-length and mantissa octant, tested through a shared
$\gcd$-chain primorial smoothness routine so that no implementation asymmetry
could enter. Approximately $1.49\times10^{9}$ candidates were tested per arm,
with $4000$ $N$-clusters per bin and cluster bootstrap resampling ($n=2000$).
The measured ratio $r(u)=p_{\mathrm{cand}}/p_{\mathrm{ctrl}}$ was:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccc}
\toprule
bin ($u$) & $r(u)$ & 95\% CI\\
\midrule
$5.96$ & $1.011$ & $[0.947,\ 1.075]$\\
$6.95$ & $0.949$ & $[0.783,\ 1.152]$\\
$7.93$ & $0.900$ & $[0.455,\ 1.700]$\\
$8.26$ & $1.200$ & $[0.500,\ 3.000]$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

All intervals cover $1$. The regression of $\log r$ on $u$ has slope $+0.036$,
CI $[-0.255,+0.345]$, $p=0.831$ --- flat. The tightest $95\%$ bound is
$|r-1|\le0.2168$ at the $u=6$ bin. Two secondary signals were real and confined
to the low-$u$ face: a per-$N$ overdispersion index $1.61\,[1.50,1.73]$ at
$u\approx6$ falling to $\approx1.00$ at $u\approx7,8$; and a Spearman correlation
$0.32$ (permutation $p=7\times10^{-4}$) between an $N$'s smoothness rate and the
fraction of factor-base primes for which $N$ is a residue, decaying to $0.04$.

This paper gives the exact arithmetic behind all three observations. Everything
below is exact and rational; no analytic estimate, error term, or asymptotic is
used in any proof.

\section{The dial and its dichotomy}

Throughout, $p$ denotes an odd prime and $\ZZ/p$ the field with $p$ elements.

\begin{definition}[Dial]
For $N\in\ZZ/p$ set
$D_p(N)=\#\{x\in\ZZ/p : x^{2}=N\}$, the number of residue classes of $x$ on
which $p\mid x^{2}-N$. A random integer has dial $1$ at every prime.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Root set]\label{lem:rootset}
For every $c\in\ZZ/p$, $\{y: y^{2}=c^{2}\}=\{c,-c\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$y^{2}=c^{2}\iff(y-c)(y+c)=0$, and $\ZZ/p$ is an integral domain.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:negself}
If $p$ is odd then $c=-c\iff c=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$c=-c$ gives $2c=0$; since $p\nmid2$, the element $2$ is invertible, so $c=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dial Dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $p$ be an odd prime. Then $D_p(c^{2})=1$ if $c=0$ and $2$ otherwise, and
$D_p(N)=0$ if and only if $N$ is not a square. Consequently $D_p(N)\le2$ for all
$N$, $D_p(0)=1$, and for $N\ne0$ one has $D_p(N)=2$ exactly when $N$ is a
quadratic residue.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:rootset} the root set of $x^{2}=c^{2}$ is $\{c,-c\}$, a
singleton exactly when $c=0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:negself} and a two-element set
otherwise. The vanishing criterion is the statement that the root set is empty
iff $N$ is not of the form $r^{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Character identity]\label{cor:char}
For odd $p$ and all $N\in\ZZ/p$, $D_p(N)=1+\chi_p(N)$, where $\chi_p$ is the
quadratic character with $\chi_p(0)=0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Check the three cases of Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} against $\chi_p(0)=0$,
$\chi_p(\text{residue})=1$, $\chi_p(\text{nonresidue})=-1$.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:char} is the conceptual pivot: the deviation of the dial from
its random value $1$ is exactly a nontrivial multiplicative character, and
everything about the first-moment null follows from the vanishing of a character
sum.

\section{Exact moments of the dial}

\begin{theorem}[First moment: exactly random]\label{thm:firstmoment}
For every prime $p$, $\displaystyle\sum_{N\in\ZZ/p}D_p(N)=p$; equivalently the
mean dial is exactly $1$, the same expected number of hit residue classes as for
a random integer.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The sum counts pairs $(x,N)$ with $x^{2}=N$. The fibres of $x\mapsto x^{2}$
partition $\ZZ/p$, so the total is $|\ZZ/p|=p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\sum_N\chi_p(N)=0$ for odd $p$ --- the classical character-sum vanishing, here a
consequence of Theorem~\ref{thm:firstmoment} via Corollary~\ref{cor:char}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Second moment]\label{thm:secondmoment}
For every odd prime $p$, $\displaystyle\sum_{N\in\ZZ/p}D_p(N)^{2}=2p-1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expanding, $\sum_N D_p(N)^{2}$ counts pairs $(x,y)$ with $x^{2}=y^{2}$, which by
fibring over $x$ equals $\sum_x D_p(x^{2})=1+2(p-1)=2p-1$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Balance]\label{cor:balance}
Exactly $(p-1)/2$ residues have $D_p=2$ and exactly $(p-1)/2$ have $D_p=0$, the
single remaining value $N=0$ having $D_p(0)=1$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
With $m_2+m_0=p-1$ and $2m_2+1=p$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:firstmoment}) we get
$m_2=(p-1)/2$.
\end{proof}

\section{The local factor and its exact variance}

For the polynomial, $\Pr_x[p\nmid f(x)]=1-D_p(N)/p$; for a random integer it is
$1-1/p$.

\begin{definition}[Local factor]
For odd $p$ and $N\in\ZZ/p$,
\[
L_p(N)=\frac{1-D_p(N)/p}{1-1/p}=\frac{p-D_p(N)}{p-1}\in\QQ .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Values and positivity]\label{prop:values}
$L_p(N)=\frac{p-2}{p-1}$ if $D_p(N)=2$, and $L_p(N)=\frac{p}{p-1}$ if
$D_p(N)=0$; $L_p(0)=1$. Since $p\ge3$ and $D_p\le2$, always $L_p(N)>0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[QR monotonicity, local form]\label{prop:localmono}
If $D_p(N)=2$ and $D_p(N')=0$ then $L_p(N)<L_p(N')$: quadratic residues are
strictly harder to make smooth at $p$ than nonresidues.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\frac{p-2}{p-1}<\frac{p}{p-1}$, the denominator being positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Local mean exactly one]\label{thm:localmean}
For every odd prime $p$, $\sum_N L_p(N)=p$, i.e.\ $\EE_N[L_p]=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\sum_N L_p(N)=\frac{1}{p-1}\bigl(p\cdot p-\sum_N D_p(N)\bigr)=\frac{p^{2}-p}{p-1}=p$
by Theorem~\ref{thm:firstmoment}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Local second moment]\label{thm:localsq}
For every odd prime $p$,
$\sum_N L_p(N)^{2}=p+\frac{1}{p-1}$, i.e.\ $\EE_N[L_p^{2}]=1+\frac{1}{p(p-1)}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\sum_N(p-D_p(N))^{2}=p^{3}-2p\sum_N D_p+\sum_N D_p^{2}=p^{3}-2p^{2}+2p-1$ by
Theorems~\ref{thm:firstmoment} and~\ref{thm:secondmoment}. Dividing by
$(p-1)^{2}$ and using $p^{3}-2p^{2}+2p-1=(p-1)(p^{2}-p+1)$ gives
$\frac{p^{2}-p+1}{p-1}=p+\frac{1}{p-1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact local variance]\label{cor:localvar}
$\Var_N(L_p)=\EE[L_p^{2}]-1=\dfrac{1}{p(p-1)}$ exactly; equivalently
$\sum_N(L_p(N)-1)^{2}=\frac{1}{p-1}$.
\end{corollary}

This exact rational variance is the entire arithmetic content of the ``advantage''
of $x^{2}-N$: a per-prime wobble of size $\Theta(p^{-2})$ around a mean pinned
exactly at the random value.

\section{The global structure correction}

Fix a finite index set $I$ and an injective family $a:I\to\{\text{odd primes}\}$.
The residue data of $N$ is $\Nbf=(N_i)_{i\in I}\in\prod_i\ZZ/a_i$; by the Chinese
remainder theorem this is the same as $N$ modulo the primorial $\prod_i a_i$, and
all statements transport along that isomorphism.

\begin{definition}[Structure correction]
$C(\Nbf)=\prod_{i\in I}L_{a_i}(N_i)\in\QQ_{>0}$: the full multiplicative
discrepancy between the heuristic smoothness probability of $x^{2}-N$ and that of
a size-matched random integer, at smoothness bound $B=\max_i a_i$. A random
integer has $C\equiv1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dispersion ceiling]
$\displaystyle\Delta(a)=\prod_{i\in I}\Bigl(1+\frac{1}{a_i(a_i-1)}\Bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The ensemble null}

\begin{theorem}[Ensemble mean exactly one]\label{thm:meanone}
For every finite family of odd primes,
\[
\sum_{\Nbf\in\prod_i\ZZ/a_i}C(\Nbf)=\prod_i a_i,
\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad \EE_{\Nbf}[C]=1\ \text{exactly.}
\]
Equivalently, averaged over a full period of $N$ modulo $\prod_i a_i$, the
structure correction is exactly $1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The sum of a product over a product index set factors:
$\sum_{\Nbf}\prod_i L_{a_i}(N_i)=\prod_i\sum_{N_i}L_{a_i}(N_i)$, and each inner
sum is $a_i$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:localmean}. The Chinese-remainder form follows
by transporting the summation index along
$\ZZ/\prod_i a_i\cong\prod_i\ZZ/a_i$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:meanone} is the exact counterpart of the experimental null
$r(u)=1$. Note what it does \emph{not} depend on: the smoothness bound $B$, the
size of $N$, the size of the values $v$, and hence $u$. There is no first-order
smoothness edge from quadratic-polynomial structure, at any scale.

\subsection{The exact variance}

\begin{theorem}[Ensemble second moment]\label{thm:ensemblesq}
$\displaystyle\sum_{\Nbf}C(\Nbf)^{2}=\prod_i\Bigl(a_i+\frac{1}{a_i-1}\Bigr)
=\Bigl(\prod_i a_i\Bigr)\Delta(a)$, so $\EE[C^{2}]=\Delta(a)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Factor as in Theorem~\ref{thm:meanone} using Theorem~\ref{thm:localsq}, then
$a_i+\frac{1}{a_i-1}=a_i\bigl(1+\frac{1}{a_i(a_i-1)}\bigr)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact ensemble variance]\label{cor:ensemblevar}
$\displaystyle\sum_{\Nbf}(C(\Nbf)-1)^{2}=\Bigl(\prod_i a_i\Bigr)(\Delta(a)-1)$,
i.e.\ $\Var(C)=\Delta(a)-1$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Expand $(C-1)^{2}=C^{2}-2C+1$ and apply Theorems~\ref{thm:meanone}
and~\ref{thm:ensemblesq} with $\#\prod_i\ZZ/a_i=\prod_i a_i$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The uniform ceiling}

\begin{lemma}[Telescoping]\label{lem:tele}
For every integer $M\ge3$, $\displaystyle\sum_{n=3}^{M}\frac{1}{n(n-1)}=\frac12-\frac1M$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\frac{1}{n(n-1)}=\frac{1}{n-1}-\frac1n$; the sum telescopes.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:half}
For any finite set $S$ of integers $\ge3$,
$\sum_{n\in S}\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\le\frac12$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$S\subseteq[3,\max S]$ and all terms are nonnegative, so the sum is at most
$\frac12-\frac{1}{\max S}<\frac12$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:tele}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:prodbound}
For nonnegative reals $x_1,\dots,x_k$ with $\sum_i x_i<1$,
$\prod_i(1+x_i)\le\bigl(1-\sum_i x_i\bigr)^{-1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. For the step it suffices that
$(1+x)\frac{1}{1-s}\le\frac{1}{1-(x+s)}$ for $x,s\ge0$, $x+s<1$, which after
clearing positive denominators reads $(1+x)(1-x-s)\le 1-s$, i.e.\ $0\le x^{2}+xs$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform dispersion ceiling]\label{thm:ceiling}
For every finite family of \emph{distinct} odd primes --- equivalently for every
smoothness bound $B$, however large ---
\[
\Delta(a)=\prod_i\Bigl(1+\frac{1}{a_i(a_i-1)}\Bigr)\le 2 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $x_i=\frac{1}{a_i(a_i-1)}\ge0$. Distinctness lets us rewrite $\sum_i x_i$ as a
sum over the \emph{set} $\{a_i\}$ of odd primes, all $\ge3$, so
$\sum_i x_i\le\frac12$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:half}. Lemma~\ref{lem:prodbound} gives
$\Delta(a)\le(1-\tfrac12)^{-1}=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Clustering is real]\label{thm:strict}
If $I\ne\emptyset$ then $\Delta(a)>1$ strictly, hence $\Var(C)>0$: the structure
correction is genuinely non-constant.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every factor is $\ge1$ and at least one is $>1$.
\end{proof}

Together these pin the phenomenon: $1<\Delta(a)\le2$ always. For the family
$\{3,5,7\}$ one computes exactly
$\Delta=\frac76\cdot\frac{21}{20}\cdot\frac{43}{42}=\frac{301}{240}\approx1.2542$,
and the infinite product over all odd primes converges to $\approx1.2967$. The
measured overdispersion index $1.61\,[1.50,1.73]$ sits inside the a-priori
ceiling of $2$.

\subsection{A tail bound}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Chebyshev inequality]\label{thm:cheb}
For every $t>0$,
\[
t^{2}\cdot\#\bigl\{\Nbf : |C(\Nbf)-1|\ge t\bigr\}\le\Bigl(\prod_i a_i\Bigr)(\Delta(a)-1).
\]
Hence the fraction of residue data with $|C-1|\ge t$ is at most
$(\Delta(a)-1)/t^{2}\le1/t^{2}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling}.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
On the exceptional set each summand $(C-1)^{2}$ is $\ge t^{2}$; sum over the set,
extend nonnegatively to the full index set, and apply
Corollary~\ref{cor:ensemblevar}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Packaged null]\label{thm:packaged}
For every finite family of distinct odd primes and every $t>0$: $\EE[C]=1$
exactly (no first-order smoothness edge), and
$\#\{\Nbf : |C(\Nbf)-1|\ge t\}\le\prod_i a_i/t^{2}$ (deviation mass controlled
uniformly in the family, hence uniformly in $B$).
\end{theorem}

This is the exact-arithmetic counterpart of the experimental verdict
``$|r-1|\le0.217$ together with a bounded per-$N$ overdispersion''.

\section{Exact joint uniformity of the dial vector}

One might hope to salvage an edge from correlations between dials at different
primes. There are none, and the statement is as sharp as possible.

\begin{theorem}[Exact independence of dials]\label{thm:uniform}
Let $a_1,\dots,a_k$ be distinct odd primes. For every prescribed pattern
$(d_1,\dots,d_k)\in\{0,2\}^{k}$,
\[
\#\bigl\{\Nbf : D_{a_i}(N_i)=d_i\ \forall i\bigr\}
=\frac{1}{2^{k}}\prod_{i=1}^{k}(a_i-1),
\]
independent of the pattern. The dial vector is exactly uniform on $\{0,2\}^{k}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The event factorises over coordinates, so the count is
$\prod_i\#\{x\in\ZZ/a_i : D_{a_i}(x)=d_i\}$; by Corollary~\ref{cor:balance} each
factor is $(a_i-1)/2$ regardless of whether $d_i$ is $0$ or $2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Extremes of $C$]\label{cor:extremes}
$C$ attains its maximum $\prod_i\frac{a_i}{a_i-1}$ exactly on the all-nonresidue
pattern and its minimum $\prod_i\frac{a_i-2}{a_i-1}$ exactly on the all-residue
pattern, and each of these sets has cardinality $2^{-k}\prod_i(a_i-1)$, a
relative density $2^{-k}\prod_i\frac{a_i-1}{a_i}$.
\end{corollary}

Extreme structure corrections are therefore exponentially rare in the number of
factor-base primes: the combinatorial reason the observed clustering is $O(1)$
rather than exponential.

\begin{theorem}[Global QR monotonicity]\label{thm:globalmono}
Let $\Nbf,\Nbf'$ agree in every coordinate except $j$, where $D_{a_j}(N_j)=2$
(residue) and $D_{a_j}(N_j')=0$ (nonresidue). Then $C(\Nbf)<C(\Nbf')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Split both products at $j$. The common tail $\prod_{i\ne j}L_{a_i}(N_i)$ is
strictly positive (Proposition~\ref{prop:values}), and
$L_{a_j}(N_j)<L_{a_j}(N_j')$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:localmono}). Multiply.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:globalmono} is the exact mechanism behind the measured Spearman
correlation $0.32$ between an $N$'s smoothness rate and its quadratic-residue
fraction: the monotone dependence is unconditional.

\section{Why the clustering dies at large $u$}

Both secondary signals disappear above $u\approx7$: the dispersion index drops
from $1.61$ to $\approx1.00$, the QR correlation from $0.32$ to $0.04$. Yet the
distribution of $C$ is completely $u$-independent --- mean exactly $1$, variance
exactly $\Delta(a)-1$. The death of the clustering therefore cannot be an
arithmetic effect. It is a counting effect, and it is exactly quantifiable.

\subsection{A finite law of total variance}

Let $\Omega$ be a finite mixing space with weights $w:\Omega\to\QQ$,
$\sum_\omega w_\omega=1$, and for each $\omega$ let $P_\omega$ be a probability
vector on a finite outcome space $K$ with value function $\val:K\to\QQ$. Write
\[
m(\omega)=\sum_k P_\omega(k)\val(k),\qquad
v(\omega)=\sum_k P_\omega(k)\bigl(\val(k)-m(\omega)\bigr)^{2},
\]
\[
\overline m=\sum_\omega w_\omega m(\omega),\qquad
\overline v=\sum_\omega w_\omega\sum_k P_\omega(k)\bigl(\val(k)-\overline m\bigr)^{2}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Law of total variance, finite and exact]\label{thm:ltv}
$\displaystyle\overline v=\sum_\omega w_\omega v(\omega)
+\sum_\omega w_\omega\bigl(m(\omega)-\overline m\bigr)^{2}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand
$(\val(k)-\overline m)^{2}=(\val(k)-m(\omega))^{2}
+2(\val(k)-m(\omega))(m(\omega)-\overline m)+(m(\omega)-\overline m)^{2}$
and sum; the cross term vanishes for each $\omega$ because
$\sum_k P_\omega(k)(\val(k)-m(\omega))=0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The dispersion identity}

Take $\Omega=\prod_i\ZZ/a_i$ with uniform weights and model the number of smooth
values found for a given $N$ as a count with
\[
m(\Nbf)=\lambda\,C(\Nbf),\qquad
v(\Nbf)=\lambda\,C(\Nbf)\bigl(1-q\,C(\Nbf)\bigr),
\]
exactly the mean and variance of a binomial count of $n$ independent trials with
success probability $q\,C(\Nbf)$, where $\lambda=nq$ is the event rate. The model
is realisable: a single Bernoulli trial with success probability $q\,C(\Nbf)$
satisfies the hypotheses with $\lambda=q$.

\begin{theorem}[Dispersion identity]\label{thm:dispid}
With $S_2=\EE[C^{2}]$ and $\EE[C]=1$,
\[
\mathrm{Mean}=\lambda,\qquad
\Var=\lambda\bigl(1+\lambda(S_2-1)-q\,S_2\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The mean is immediate from $\EE[C]=1$. For the variance apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:ltv}: the average conditional variance is
$\lambda\EE[C]-\lambda q\EE[C^{2}]=\lambda-\lambda qS_2$, and the variance of the
conditional means is
$\lambda^{2}\EE[C^{2}]-2\lambda^{2}\EE[C]+\lambda^{2}=\lambda^{2}(S_2-1)$. Add.
\end{proof}

Dividing, the dispersion index is exactly $1+\lambda(S_2-1)-qS_2$. The arithmetic
enters only through $S_2$, which equals $\Delta(a)$ and is at most $2$; the
observable excess over Poisson is proportional to the event rate $\lambda$.

\begin{theorem}[Bound on excess dispersion]\label{thm:excess}
If $\lambda,q\ge0$ and $S_2\ge1$ then
$|\Var-\mathrm{Mean}|\le\lambda\bigl(\lambda(S_2-1)+qS_2\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\Var-\mathrm{Mean}=\lambda(\lambda(S_2-1)-qS_2)$, and the absolute value of a
difference of two nonnegative quantities is at most their sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Decay of clustering for $x^{2}-N$]\label{thm:decay}
For any family of distinct odd primes, in the model above,
\[
\bigl|\Var-\mathrm{Mean}\bigr|\le\lambda(\lambda+2q)=\mathrm{Mean}\cdot(\lambda+2q).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:excess} with $S_2=\Delta(a)$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:ensemblesq}), $\Delta(a)\le2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling}) and
$\Delta(a)\ge1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:strict}): $\lambda(\Delta-1)\le\lambda$ and
$q\Delta\le2q$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Reading the experiment}

The bound on excess dispersion is proportional to the event rate and uniform in
the smoothness bound. At $u\approx6$ the experiment had $\lambda$ of order one and
observed a dispersion index $\approx1.61$, comfortably below the ceiling. At
$u\approx8$ smooth events were so rare --- of order $18$ events across $4000$
$N$-clusters, i.e.\ $\lambda\approx0.005$ --- that Theorem~\ref{thm:decay} forces
$|D-1|\le\lambda+2q\approx0.005+2q$, well below any achievable resolution. The
clustering did not die; it became unobservable, at a rate the theory predicts
exactly. The same reasoning applies to the QR correlation: detecting a monotone
dependence of a per-$N$ rate on a per-$N$ covariate requires several events per
$N$, and at $\lambda=0.005$ almost every $N$ has zero events.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Exact dial spectrum}
Given an odd prime $p$, initialise a length-$p$ counter array to zero and, for
each $x\in\{0,\dots,p-1\}$, increment the entry at $x^{2}\bmod p$. Cost $O(p)$
time and space. The counters must be $1$ at index $0$ and lie in $\{0,2\}$
elsewhere, with $(p-1)/2$ of each value; the first moment must be $p$ and the
second $2p-1$. This is a direct check of
Theorems~\ref{thm:dichotomy},~\ref{thm:firstmoment},~\ref{thm:secondmoment} and
Corollary~\ref{cor:balance}.

\subsection{Exact dispersion ceiling}
Given a list of distinct odd primes, compute
$\Delta(a)=\prod(1+\frac{1}{p(p-1)})$ in exact rational arithmetic, at cost
$O(k)$ rational multiplications with numerator and denominator growing to
$O(k\log B)$ bits. The output must satisfy $1<\Delta\le2$
(Theorems~\ref{thm:ceiling},~\ref{thm:strict}), and equal exactly $301/240$ for
$\{3,5,7\}$.

\subsection{Brute-force ensemble verification}
Given a small family of distinct odd primes, enumerate all $\prod a_i$ residue
tuples, compute $C$ exactly as a rational and accumulate. Cost
$O(k\prod_i a_i)$. The first sum must equal exactly $\prod a_i$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:meanone}), the second exactly $(\prod a_i)\Delta(a)$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:ensemblesq}), and each of the $2^{k}$ dial patterns must occur
exactly $2^{-k}\prod(a_i-1)$ times (Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform}). These are
zero-tolerance identity checks, not statistical tests.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Relation to earlier measurements}
An earlier study reported candidate/control smoothness ratios of $0.88$--$0.91$
for $u<4.75$ at scales up to $2^{44}$ and attributed them to a finite-$x$
correction to the Dickman heuristic \emph{shared with the controls}, i.e.\ to the
claim that the relation pool ensemble-equals an unrestricted random pool. The
present work extends that claim to $u\le8.5$ and is fully consistent with it; no
novelty is claimed against it. What is new is the exact mechanism, which turns
the empirical null into an identity, and the uniform ceiling, which converts ``we
found no edge'' into ``no edge of any size is possible in the first moment, and
any second-moment effect is at most a factor $\Delta\le2$''.

\subsection{Three structural patterns}
\emph{(i) Every structure signal here is a second-moment phenomenon.} The first
moment is pinned to the random value by a character sum that vanishes
identically; this is not a near-cancellation with a small error term but an exact
identity for every prime.

\emph{(ii) The second moment is a convergent Euler product, hence $O(1)$.} The
polynomial structure of $x^{2}-N$ can never buy more than a constant factor, and
that constant is at most $2$, with true value $\approx1.2967$ in the limit of all
odd primes.

\emph{(iii) Observability of a second moment is proportional to the event rate.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:decay} says any experiment reporting overdispersion at rate
$\lambda$ is reporting $\lambda\Var(C)$ and nothing else. Comparing dispersion
measured at high rate with dispersion at low rate and concluding the mechanism
changed is a methodological error: the mechanism is constant, the microscope is
not.

\subsection{Consequences for sieve practice}
The practical reading is deflationary. The quadratic sieve's efficiency comes
from the smallness of $|x^{2}-N|$ near $\sqrt N$ and from restricting the factor
base to primes with $\left(\frac Np\right)=1$ --- a cost saving in the sieve loop,
not a probability gain in the smoothness rate. Any attempt to extract an
asymptotic improvement from the arithmetic shape of the polynomial is bounded a
priori by a factor under $1.30$ in the second moment and by $1$ exactly in the
first. The per-$N$ variance is nonetheless real: by Theorem~\ref{thm:globalmono},
$N$ with a low quadratic-residue fraction across the factor base have
systematically larger structure corrections. In multiplier-selection variants,
where one chooses $k$ so that $kN$ has favourable residue properties,
Theorems~\ref{thm:uniform}--\ref{thm:globalmono} give the exact distribution of
what is being optimised and Corollary~\ref{cor:extremes} quantifies how rare the
best multipliers are.

\subsection{Limitations}
The exact theorems are unconditional for every finite family of odd primes. The
experimental verdict carries two disclosed truncations: with $N\le2^{80}$ and
$x\le4\sqrt N$ at $B=1000$ the reachable $u$ tops out near $8.5$, so
production-scale $u\ge9$ was not measured (though the theorems apply there
verbatim); and the run was time-capped, so the $u\approx7$ and $u\approx8$ bins
had tiny event counts ($18$ vs $20$, $12$ vs $10$) and correspondingly wide
intervals. Finally, the theorems describe heuristic \emph{local densities};
converting them into a rigorous statement about actual smoothness counts of
$x^{2}-N$ over a range of $x$ requires a sieve-theoretic input not supplied here.

\section{Future work}

\begin{conjecture}[Degree-$d$ dispersion ceiling]
For an irreducible $f\in\ZZ[X]$ of degree $d$ with Galois group $G$, the ensemble
second moment of the structure correction over residue data is
\[
\prod_p\Bigl(1+\frac{\Var_G(\fix)}{(p-1)^{2}}+O(p^{-3/2})\Bigr),
\]
where $\Var_G(\fix)$ is the variance of the number of fixed points of a uniform
element of $G$; in particular it is bounded by an explicit constant $c(d)$, and
reduces to factors $1+\frac{1}{p(p-1)}$ exactly when $G=S_2$.
\end{conjecture}

The ceiling above came entirely from the variance of the number of roots of the
sieve polynomial mod $p$, and for degree $d$ that variance is governed by the
Chebotarev distribution of Frobenius cycle types. The \emph{mean} number of roots
is $1$ for every irreducible $f$ (Burnside's lemma applied to the transitive
$G$-action on the roots), so the first-order null survives at every degree.

\emph{Vanishing-rate criterion for observable clustering.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:decay} bounds excess dispersion by $\lambda+2q$; this should be
sharpened into a two-sided criterion giving, for a target power and a number of
clusters, the minimum event rate at which an overdispersion of a given size is
detectable. Such a criterion would let future smoothness experiments
pre-register the $u$ range in which a clustering claim is even meaningful.

\emph{Number-field sieve analogue.} There the relevant polynomial has degree $d$
and the local factors involve the splitting type of $p$ in the associated number
field. The same three patterns should recur, with the ceiling determined by the
Galois group; quantifying it would give an a-priori bound on how much polynomial
selection can buy.

\emph{Beyond the second moment.} Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform} shows the dial vector
is exactly uniform on $\{0,2\}^{k}$, so $\log C$ is an exact sum of independent
bounded random variables. Its full distribution should be computable, giving
exact large-deviation rates for the structure correction and hence a sharp
replacement for Theorem~\ref{thm:cheb}.

